issue_context.body:
Hi, I am maintaining the openssl bindings for the R programming language. When upgrading to OpenSSLv3 we see some tests failing because they were using deprecated hashing algorithms md4 and ripemd160.

I suppose this is expected, however the way these are failing is weird. `EVP_get_digestbyname` seems to succeed, even for deprecated functions. `EVP_Digest` then fails, but with a very strange message ("failed fetch").


Below an example program that hashes the word "hello" with the algo given in arg[1]. For example:

```sh
./testalgo md5
# 5d41402abc4b2a76b9719d911017c592

./testalgo md4
#OpenSSL error in (null): fetch failed
````


I am also seeing the "failed fetch" error in other errors like [this one](https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/12297). It may be very helpful to users to get a more specific error message why an algo is unavailable



```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <openssl/err.h>
#include <openssl/hmac.h>

void raise_error(){
  unsigned long err = ERR_get_error(); 
  ERR_clear_error(); 
  printf("OpenSSL error in %s: %s\n", ERR_func_error_string(err), ERR_reason_error_string(err));
  exit(1);
}

int main( int argc, const char* argv[] ){
  OpenSSL_add_all_digests();
  OpenSSL_add_all_algorithms();
  OpenSSL_add_all_ciphers();
  ERR_load_crypto_strings();
  const char *algo = argc > 1 ? argv[1] : "md5";
  const EVP_MD *md = EVP_get_digestbyname(algo);
  if(md == NULL){
    printf("Failed to find digest algo %s\n", algo);
    raise_error();
  }
  unsigned int out_len;
  unsigned char out[EVP_MAX_MD_SIZE];
  int res = EVP_Digest("hello", 5, out, &out_len, md, NULL);
  if(res == 0){
    raise_error();
  }
  for (int i = 0; i < out_len; i++) {
    printf("%x", out[i]);
  }
  printf("\n");
}
```
